{"name":"Facial icing with green tea ice cubes","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Iced_tea_with_ice_cubes.jpg/960px-Iced_tea_with_ice_cubes.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iced_tea_with_ice_cubes.jpg","caption":"Iced tea with ice cubes","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["Facial icing with green tea ice cubes is a social media skincare routine: brewed green tea is frozen into cubes and rubbed over the face to reduce puffiness and calm skin. Users report a cooler, tighter, less puffy look afterward.","Cold constricts blood vessels and slows local blood flow, which is the proposed route to less swelling, and green tea adds polyphenols. General cold therapy is agreed to relieve symptoms like swelling, with conflicting study results, mostly in injuries. Green tea compounds have no proven biological effects in humans, and no study has tested this routine."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["Users brew green tea, freeze it in an ice tray, wrap a cube in a cloth or glide it over the face in circles for a short time, usually in the morning. The effect is described as temporary."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Ice burns and skin irritation are possible, especially with prolonged contact. Circulation disorders are listed contraindications for cold therapy."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Iced_tea_with_ice_cubes.jpg/960px-Iced_tea_with_ice_cubes.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Iced_tea_with_ice_cubes.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Iced tea with ice cubes","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","creator":"Editor at Large","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/